<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Spanish Haiti</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1822 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first, nine-week independence of the Dominican east of Hispaniola, declared in 1821 and ended by Haitian occupation in 1822.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Frustrated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain's</a> neglect during <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/espana-boba">España Boba</a>, the lawyer and official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a> led a bloodless coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> on 1 December 1821. He proclaimed the Independent State of Spanish <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a> and sought union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">Núñez de Cáceres</a> headed a provisional government and issued a constitutive act modelled on the Spanish American republics. The new state kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and the existing social hierarchy, which alienated many black and mulatto Dominicans, and some frontier towns declared for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a> instead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a>'s government never replied to the request for union.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-pierre-boyer">Jean-Pierre Boyer</a>, president of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti</a>, marched east with an army, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> surrendered without resistance on 9 February 1822. The whole island was united under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a> until 1844, and the period is remembered in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> as the 'Ephemeral Independence'.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-spanish-haiti.jpg" alt="Republic of Spanish Haiti" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spanish_Haiti.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Núñez de Cáceres</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estado Independiente del Haití Español, Ephemeral Independence</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1822&amp;focus=republic-of-spanish-haiti" aria-label="Republic of Spanish Haiti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Spanish Haiti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L3105 1749M3105 1749L2945 2261M3105 1749L3041 1746"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3105 1749h0M2945 2261h0M3041 1746h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1822&amp;focus=republic-of-spanish-haiti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
